<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liao dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Khitan Liao, Liao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Liao dynasty was a state founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> that ruled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> from the tenth to the early twelfth century. It was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>'s principal northern rival, and the Treaty of Chanyuan in 1005 obliged the Song to make annual payments to the Liao. The Liao was overthrown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> Jin in the 1120s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty.jpg" alt="Liao dynasty" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liao_circuits.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Liao state grew from the consolidation of power among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> in the 8th century and their expansionist campaigns in the latter half of the 9th century. The Yila chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-liao">Abaoji</a>, born in 872, became leader of the Khitans and proclaimed a Chinese-style dynastic state. The Khitans had elected their leaders since the time of the Dahe Confederation, and the new rulers drew on that inheritance: the Rebirth Ceremony, performed every 12 years, confirmed the emperor's right to rule.</p><p>The state was officially the Great Khitan State, renamed Great Liao by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-liao">Abaoji</a>'s successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-deguang">Emperor Taizong of Liao</a>, changed back to Great Khitan in 983 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shengzong-of-liao">Emperor Shengzong of Liao</a>, and restored as Great Liao in 1066 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-daozong-of-liao">Emperor Daozong of Liao</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Liao conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a>, including present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, by fuelling a proxy war that led to the collapse of the Later Tang. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-zhou">Later Zhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-song">Emperor Taizong of Song</a> annexed Northern Han in 979 and then invaded Liao, but lost a pitched battle on the Gaoliang River and fled wounded in a donkey cart. Under Shengzong, who came to the throne aged 11, his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-yanyan">Xiao Yanyan</a> ruled as regent until her death in 1009 and commanded an army of her own.</p><p>The Liao attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a> in 1004, and after heavy fighting both sides agreed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyuan-treaty">Chanyuan Treaty</a>, which made them peers and began about 120 years of peace. The dynasty ended in 1125, when the Jurchen-led Jin captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-tianzuo-of-liao">Emperor Tianzuo of Liao</a>. Yelü Dashi, a survivor of the house, led loyalists west and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a>, or Western Liao, which ruled parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> for almost a century before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> conquered it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Liao ran two governments in parallel: a Northern Administration for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> and other nomadic peoples, and a Southern Administration for the settled Han population. At its height the state held what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin">Jilin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heilongjiang">Heilongjiang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, with much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and northern parts of the Korean peninsula. Its peak population is estimated at 750,000 Khitans and two to three million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a>.</p><p>Khitan women hunted and managed herds, finances and property while men were at war, and elite women such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulu-ping">Shulü Ping</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-yanyan">Xiao Yanyan</a> took part in military affairs. Religion combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, Daoism and Khitan tribal belief, with imperial patronage mostly going to Buddhism. A complete Tripitaka was finished in 1075. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> buried their elite dead in metal masks and woven-wire suits.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Liao dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Khitan Liao</b> state absorbed most of its territory and employed former Balhae officials, refugees strengthened the new Korean kingdom of Goryeo, and the Jurchens, who later founded the Jin dynasty, descended from Mohe peoples of the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/balhae">Balhae · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its greatest early test came from the Khitan <b>Liao dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jin dynasty was founded by the Jurchen, a Tungusic people of the forests and river valleys of Manchuria who lived by farming, hunting, fishing and stock-raising and paid tribute to the Khitan <b>Liao dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qara Khitai was the western remnant of the <b>Liao dynasty</b> of the Khitans, a Sinicized Para-Mongolic people of eastern Mongolia and north-east China, whose empire the Jurchen-led Jin dynasty conquered between 1114 and 1125.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It never recovered the Sixteen Prefectures around modern Beijing, which the Khitan <b>Liao dynasty</b> held, and under the Chanyuan Treaty of 1005 it agreed to costly annual payments of silk and silver to the Liao.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1120s the Song allied with the Jurchen, former subjects of the <b>Liao</b> who had founded the Jin dynasty, and together they conquered the Liao by 1125.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
